{"hash":"a80b72d12a671bd1","voice":"en-IN-NeerjaExpressiveNeural","rate":"+4%","dur":104.592,"said":"You have reached Shri Omkareshwar. Look closely: the Jyotirlinga is a small, natural stone, set low, and it is said to be always surrounded by water. Devotees offer only water here, like the Narmada water you watched the pilgrims carry up from the ghat, and they do not touch it. This is the Lord who stayed for a mountain’s devotion and blessed a king, on an island shaped like Om. Fold your hands. Adi Shankaracharya, who by tradition found his own guru on this very island, sings of this place in the Dwadasha Jyotirlinga Stotram: ‘Kaverikanarmadayoh pavitre samagame sajjanataranaya, sadaiva Mandhatripure vasantam Omkaram isham Shivam ekam ide.’ ‘At the holy meeting of the Kaveri and the Narmada, to carry good souls across, he dwells for ever in the city of Mandhata: I praise that One, the Lord Omkara, Shiva.’ Omkareshwar is the fourth Jyotirlinga of the Stotram. Tradition holds that darshan here is complete only with darshan of Mamleshwar, Amaleshwar, across the Narmada; the Stotram itself names them together, Omkaram Amaleshwaram. Pilgrims may visit the twelve Jyotirlingas in any order their journey allows; by tradition, the yatra begins at Somnath, the first, and is held complete with darshan at Grishneshwar, the twelfth, near Ellora. Let the drums and bells begin, and let the lamps be raised. Om Namah Shivaya.\n\nDid you know? Each night after the Shayan Aarti, a silver swing, a bed and a chaupad game are set out in the sanctum, for Shiva and Parvati are believed to come here to rest.","segments":[{"key":"text","start":0,"end":1332},{"key":"didYouKnow","start":1348,"end":1508}],"words":[[0.098,0.18,0,3],[0.29,0.252,4,8],[0.555,0.373,9,16],[0.939,0.337,17,21],[1.288,0.913,22,33],[3.101,0.204,35,39],[3.317,0.601,40,47],[4.135,0.108,49,52],[4.255,0.769,53,64],[5.036,0.12,65,67],[5.168,0.072,68,69],[5.252,0.517,70,75],[5.974,0.529,77,84],[6.514,0.481,85,90],[7.212,0.312